particular in the last lecture we have taken a detailed look at sinusoidal positional encoding and in the lecture before that we have taken a look at integer and binary position encoding so you might be thinking why are we spending this time on positional encoding in a deep seek from scratch series the main reason is that if you take a look at the deep seek paper they have this multi-head latent attention mechanism right we have already covered the most basic version of the mla but deep seek r1 and deep seek v3 which were released in 2025 they combine multi-head latent attention with a special type of positional embedding which is called as the rotary positional encoding if you see over here the mla is combined with rotary positional encoding so without understanding rotary positional encoding it''s impossible for us to understand how it''s combined with multi-head latent attention and that''s why we have been spending the past few lectures on understanding different types of positional encoding now we have built up sufficient knowledge to finally tackle what exactly is rotary positional encoding so let''s get started with today''s lecture if you recall towards the end of the previous lecture we saw some limitations with sinusoidal embeddings one major limitation was in sinusoidal embeddings we added the positional encoding values directly to the token embedding right or token encoding and we know that the token encoding essentially captures the semantics so by adding an information about the position directly to the semantics we are polluting the semantic information carried by the token embeddings ideally what i want is the follows right so let''s take a look at the entire llm architecture and i''m going to copy this and paste it over here right this is the entire llm architecture currently if you see here you can see that the positional encoding and the positional embeddings are not the same so what we are doing with sinusoidal position encoding is that we are adding we are adding token encodings to the positional encoding so here is where the positional embeddings or positional encodings come into the picture ideally i don''t want this ideally i want my token embeddings to be passed unfiltered to the transformer block so that their semantic meaning is not diluted and then the question which we asked towards the end of previous lecture is that why don''t we include the token position in the attention mechanism itself instead of adding the positional embeddings to the token embeddings in this first data pre-processing step why don''t we include information about the positional embedding in the multi-head attention mechanism and that''s where rotary positional encoding idea is born so what we saw in the previous lecture was that if you take a look at the multi-head attention mechanism right you can see that the positional embeddings are not the same as the positional embeddings so that''s why we are adding the positional embeddings to the positional embeddings and that''s why we are adding the positional embeddings to the positional embeddings so that''s why we are adding the positional embeddings to the positional embeddings and that''s why we are adding the positional embeddings to the positional embeddings so that''s why we are adding the positional embeddings to the positional embeddings so that''s why we are adding the positional embeddings to the positional embeddings and that''s why we are adding the positional embeddings to the positional embeddings so that''s information into the query vectors and the key vectors that''s the first question the second question which we asked in the end of the previous lecture was that if you see the token embeddings are just added to the positional embeddings right the token embeddings are added to the positional embeddings over here in this step which has also been shown over here the token embeddings are added to the positional embeddings which changes the magnitude of the token embedding itself which is not good ideally what i want is i don''t want the magnitude of the original vector to change so let''s say this is my original query vector instead of adding another vector to it so instead of adding another vector and forming a new vector why don''t i rotate this vector if i want to capture information or if i want to inject information about the position why don''t i take a query vector or the key vector and simply rotate it by an angle theta to inject the information about position you , so if a token has a higher position i''ll rotate it by a different angle if it has a or if it''s if it has a position which is smaller or position index or it''s at a lower position i''ll rotate it by a smaller angle essentially the angle you will quantify some information about my position but the magnitude of the vector will not change since i''m just doing the rotation the magnitude of the query vector or the key vector is not going to change that will make sure that my original vector magnitude remains the same so there are these two ideas with which we start the today''s lecture first instead of adding the positional embedding in the data pre-processing block over here why don''t i add it in the attention mechanism in particular why don''t i modify the query and the key vectors second instead of just adding my positional encoding vector to the query and the key vector why don''t i just rotate these vectors so that the magnitude of the original vector remains unchanged with these two ideas we start our lecture today on understanding rotary positional encoding now if you understand rotary positional encoding you''ll never forget it because it''s a very visual concept but if you directly go into the mathematics it might sound hard or even intimidating or scary at first so i''m going to try to make this lecture as visual as possible okay let''s get started so the main idea of rotary positional encoding is to take my query and the key vectors and to apply s