g to depend on the index okay so what is this index over here as we have seen previously every positional encoding vector will have the index equal to what''s the embedding dimension so the index goes from zero to the embedding dimension and the position p uh goes from zero to the context size in the current case what we have chosen my number of uh uh the number of positions or the number of tokens in the input sequence are equal to five and the dimension i''m choosing is equal to four the dimension i''m choosing is equal to four so this i is such that this i is such that for the first two indices it''s equal to zero and for the later two indices it''s equal to one for the two indexes it''s equal to one for the two indexes two later two indexes after that it will be equal to two etc so there will be groups of two each um okay so x1 and x2 that''s x1 and x2 that''s my first pair i rotate it by an angle theta and i get my x1 dash so what is x1 dash x1 dash is now the x coordinate of my rotated vector x2 dash is the y coordinate of my rotated vector that''s how i get my new that''s how i get my new values which are x1 dash and x2 dash so do you observe something over here one thing to note here is that i added or i injected information about the position which is quantified by my theta over here but nowhere did i change the magnitude of the original vector right if you take a look at the original vector here i just have i have rotated it so the magnitude of the new vector is going to be unchanged that is going to be the same that''s how i get my x1 dash and x2 dash and this is the same operation which we do for the third index and the fourth index so for the third index and the fourth index you see x3 and x4 that forms a vector over here x3 and x4 that''s this vector and this vector i''m going to take this vector and i''m going to rotate this vector also now and what will be the angle i''m going to rotate this vector also and what will be this vector i''m going to rotate this vector also and what will be this angle with which this vector is now rotated that is given by same formula omega i into p so p remains the same because both of these x1 x2 x3 x4 are for the first token but what changes is i so i was 0 for x1 and x2 right i is going to be 1 for x3 and x4 uh so then i''m going to rotate this and i get my new vector whose x coordinate is going to be x3 dash and whose y coordinate is going to be x4 dash so that''s how i get my x3 dash and x4 dash again the important thing to note here is that the magnitude although x3 dash is different than x3 x4 dash is different than x4 that''s fine but the magnitude of this new vector the magnitude of this new vector is the same as the original vector the magnitude of x3 x4 is the same as the magnitude of the vector x3 dash x4 dash which is these two vectors they have the same magnitude that''s the key point to note over here so i''m going to rotate this vector over here and i''m going to rotate this vector over here so now all of this is shown in one diagram over here if you see what is done is that you take the original query vector or you take any query or the key vector and you look at a particular position and you split it into groups right now two two indices together will form one vector and you rotate you rotate you rotate that vector you rotate that vector you rotate that vector by some angle and that angle with which we rotate every vector encodes something about the position because that angle is given by p divided by ten thousand raise to two i divided by d so the higher the position the higher is this angle going to be and the higher the index the lower the angle is going to be so for a given position if you take a look at the for a given position p uh as my index increases the amount of rotation further decreases we''ll we''ll see about that the rotation in one of the next parts but for now this one figure actually summarizes everything which we have done we take the query vect