d side and it comes with a bad side first let me recap what is the kv cache why do we need it what is its good side and what is its bad side so it all starts by looking at the inference pipeline of language models and this is how language models do inference we first have an input sequence of tokens let''s say we have four tokens the next day is and then we have to predict the next token that''s the main task of language model inference this input sequence goes through this entire architecture it goes through this first block of data pre-processing it goes through the transformer block it goes through the output layer and then we get a logits matrix through which the next token is predicted one key thing to realize here is that to predict the next token we only need the context vector of this last token so let me explain to you how once we are in the multi-head attention layer we get the attention score between this last token and all the other tokens and that''s how we can get the context vector for this last token this context vector then goes through all of these remaining layers it goes through these output layers and ultimately we get a logits vector corresponding to is which is the last token in my input sequence this logits vector is a vector which has fifty thousand tokens if or fifty thousand parameters if the vocabulary size is fifty thousand and then i choose that index which has the highest value or the highest probability and then i find the token corresponding to this index and that''s my next token prediction which is bright so the first key insight to remember during llm inference is that to get the next token prediction i only need the context vector of my last token of the input sequence that''s it i don''t need this other context vectors at all that''s the first insight the second insight is that let''s say uh the next day is was the input and bright is the output now this bright is appended to my input and that becomes my new input sequence so the new input sequence is the next day is bright which will again pass through this entire architecture and we predict the new token so let''s look at what happens when this new input matrix passes through the transformer block especially let''s look at what happens when this new input matrix passes through the multi-head attention block so here we see that we''ll get the queries matrix we''ll get the keys matrix we''ll get the values matrix we''ll get the attention scores we''ll get the attention weights and we''ll get the context matrix but if you pay close attention look at these black boxes which i have marked i have marked a black box over here i have marked a black box over here i have marked a black box over here here here and in the context matrix also so the second key realization is that all of these black boxes are already computed in my previous iteration in my previous iteration in the multi-head attention i have already computed all of these queries keys values except for this last row over here that''s corresponding to my new token so i have not computed that so the second realization is that i''m doing so many recalculations here can i store something that''s where the idea of the cache comes into the picture so then we started backtracking to predict the next token so what i have to do is to predict the next token based on these inputs right what do i need to predict the next token i only need the context vector corresponding to the last token so to get that context vector what i only need is that i need the attention weights for my last token multiplied by the values matrix to get the attention weights i need the attention scores to get the attention scores i need the query vector multiplied by the keys now all of these things which i''ve marked in the black boxes are cached which means to get the keys matrix i will cache my previous keys i will cache my previous values and i will only compute this new row i will only compute these new rows so whenever a new whenever a new token comes in all i have to do is that i have to find the query vector corresponding to the new token i have to find the keys vector corresponding to the new token and append it to the cache keys so that gives me the total keys matrix and the third thing i have to do is i have to find the value vector for the new token and i have to append it for the cast values then i multiply queries with keys transpose i get the attention scores from that i get the attention weights i multiply it with the values matrix and that''s how i get the context vector only for the last token this context vector for the last token will traverse the rest of this journey and then it will predict my next token so you see what we have done here we don''t recompute the values and the keys again at all we store them from the previous iteration that''s the idea of caching we cache the keys and values now as we have seen in the previous lecture what happens with caching is that it reduces the number of computations which we need to do so if you see this line when we implement caching the amount of computations which we need to do increase only linearly with the number of input tokens however if you don''t do caching the amount of computations increase quadrately so that''s one good side of implementing k value cache the good side is that it reduces the number of computations which we need to do that ultimately reduces our cost that''s a good thing for us then what''s the bad side the bad side or the ugly side of the k value cache is that we or the key value cache is that we are storing something now we are caching something and just like every piece of land to own every piece of land we have to pay a price right similarly for every piece of data stored in the memory for every single parameter stored in the memory we have to pay a price the number of parameters you need to store when you look at a k value cache key value cache is given by this much why is it this much well because let''s look at one transformer block initially if you look at one transformer block let''s say i have four tokens the next day is let''s say this is my four tokens and each token has a dimension of four now these dimension is the number of attention heads multiplied by the head dimension so that''s n into h the number of rows over here is equal to my context size so that''s s and this is just one batch but if i''m processing in multiple batches then this should be multiplied by b that''s where the b into n into s h into s comes in these are the number of parameters which need to be stored and remember if this is the keys uh if this is the keys matrix there will be a values matrix along with it so then this will be multiplied with two uh and then i''m just showing this for one transformer block there are usually multiple transformer block in language models so that will be multiplied by l which is the number of transformer blocks and this one more two additional is the number of bytes per floating point so i''m assuming that each parameter stored is 16 bits which is two bytes so the size of the kv cache is given by l into b into n into h into s into 2 into 2. now if you have gpt2 that''s 36 mb of memory if you have gpt3 that''s 4.5 gb of memory but if you have deep sync r1 or their v3 base model which essentially uh has 61 transformer blocks 128 attention heads and each head having 128 dimensions context size of 100 000 the kv cache size is 400 gb that''s a huge size now what happens with this huge size is that your memory is overloaded and for remember for every piece of memory occupied by data you have to pay a price so that would increase our price during inference because the company running this also has to incur more cost so this is usually disadvantageous to us the more memory which is stored our overall computations will also become slow and the thing is as the parameter size increases of the model rate so here as i go from the model is becoming more and more complex as the parameter size of the model increases the size of the k value cache just goes on increasing that''s actually shown by this red curve over here so key value cache speeds things that''s the good good side but it takes space that''s the dark side or the ugly side i hope in this much time i have provided a summary of what the key value cache memory problem really is so the memory problem needs to be solved because of course we cannot have memory being occupied all the time that would slow computations that would cost us more as a company and so i''ll charge my clients also more deep seek costs very less during inference time so of course they did not go with this directly they made a number of innovations and their main innovation is the multi-head latent attention but to get to that we first need to understand innovations which came before that and the first major innovation which came before that is the multi-query attention the rest of this lecture we are going to visually understand the multi-query attention its advantages and disadvantages and then we are going to implement it in code so let''s get started the way i think about multi-query attention is that what is the simplest thing which you can do to solve the kv cache memory problem and multi-query attention solves this problem by asking the question what if all the attention heads share the same key and the value matrices so if you just look at this statement it feels difficult to visualize this okay what does it mean attention heads sharing the same key and value matrices so i have constructed some visuals for you to understand this a lot better so first let''s look at normal multi-head attention without multi-query attention in normal multi-head attention we start with an input embedding matrix let''s say i have five tokens the next day is bright each token let''s say has eight dimensions that''s the embedding dimension we multiply the input embedding matrix with the keys and key''s trainable weight matrix and the values trainable weight matrix now if you look at the keys weight matrix let''s say there are four heads if there are four attention heads the keys weight matrix is split into four parts the first is the keys weight matrix for attention head one the second is the keys weight matrix for attention head two the third is the keys weight matrix for attention head three and the fourth is the keys weight matrix for attention head three and the fourth is the keys weight matrix for attention head four that''s the similar for the values or that''s what happens for the values weight matrix also the first part or the first split is for the first attention head second for the second head third for the third attention head and the fourth for the fourth attention head now normally in normal multi-head attention you see i have shown all of these head colors with different values right this is shown in a different color this is shown in a different color the reason i have shown in different colors is because naturally the weights of the keys for different attention heads are different why would they be the same they are initialized in random manner similarly the weights for let''s say for this attention head and for the second attention head the values weights are different from each other and that''s why all of these are shown in different colors and naturally now when you take keys weight matrix and values weight matrix like this and multiply it with the input embedding matrix you get the keys matrix and the values matrix right for different heads and here also since the wk and we are different for every attention head the final keys matrix is also different for head one compared to head two compared to head three compared to head four and the final values matrix is also different for head one and head two and head three and head four now if you see very closely the number of dimensions in the key value cache the number of dimensions of the key value cache is directly dependent on the number of attention heads which we have and every head will have a certain dimension right so if if let''s say uh take a look at this keys matrix and if it generated during one iteration of my inference i will have to cache this entire keys matrix so that means i have to cache the keys matrix for head one i have to cache the keys matrix for head two i have to cache the keys matrix for head three and i have to cache the keys matrix for head four what multi-query attention does is that it says what if i have to cache just the keys for one head and i don''t cache the keys for the other heads then you might be saying okay that will not work because all of these other heads have different values you need to cache them multi-query attention then says what if all of the attention heads has have the same values as the head number one so if you look at the keys matrix what if head one head two head three and head four all have the same values such as like this if you look at the values matrix what if head one head two head three and head four all have the same values such as i''m showing right now so that actually means that if you look at the trainable keys matrix and trainable value matrix here also head one head two head three and head four will have the same values so among these different heads the values are same that''s why i''ve shown it with the different color within one particular head the values can change but if i showed them with a different color it would be too many colors so that''s why for the sake of simplicity within one head also i''ve shown with the same color but the real point i''m trying to illustrate here is that for head one whatever the values are there for head one it''s the same for head two head three and head four and that''s exactly the same for the values weight matrix whatever values we have for the head one it''s the same for head two head three and head four then if you think about it then i just need to cache it for one head right i just need to cache the keys and the values for one head i don''t need to cache for all the other heads because they are the same values so why should i store more things in my memory uh all of these head two head three and head four are the same values so that''s why what happens in multi-query attention is that if you look at the size of the kv cache it depends on the number of attention heads right now this is taken out of the picture completely this is taken out of the picture completely because we get rid of we get rid of the differences in the heads which means that now i only need to store the keys and the values for one head so in in the case of multi-query attention the size of the kv cache size or the size of the kv cache suddenly reduces by a factor of n and if you have 32 heads or if you have 128 heads like in the case of d deep seek deep seek has 128 heads so multi-query attention reduces the kv cache size by a factor of 128 that''s a huge reduction that much is the reduction in the memory space if you use multi-query attention so let me re revisit what i just mentioned what i just told you is a simple trick i told you that instead of having different wk and wv values for different heads what if you just have it for one head and then you create copies for head two head three and head four so if you create copies in wk and we even in the keys matrix what will happen is that you just get the keys uh matrix for head one and you create copies for head two head three and head four so even if you create the values for head one and you create copies for head two head three and head four that is what is actually meant by what if all attention heads share the same key and the value matrices right now what this ultimately leads to is that it leads to the fact that the size of the kv cache reduces by a factor of n because i don''t have to store the keys and the values for all my heads right now i just store it for one head that''s it