 to cache the keys and the value matrices during inference this is called as the kv cache now the kv cache has a lot of advantages one major advantage of the kv cache being that if you plot the amount of computations needed versus the number of input tokens if you don''t have a kv cache the amount of computations needed increase quadratically because you''re repeating the calculations each time right but if you have a kv cache the amount of computations which you need don''t grow quadratically with the number of input tokens they only grow linearly which means that even if you''re doing more and more and more and more inference since you''re caching you are not increasing the cost quadratically you''re only increasing linearly so you actually save uh so you actually save this much amount of recomputations and that speeds up inference which is actually good we speed up inference due to the kv cache and it speeds up inference by a huge magnitude but what is the dark side of the kv cache the kv cache actually comes with a dark side and that dark side is that it takes up memory now remember these black boxes which i mentioned over here we need to cache them which means we need to store them in the memory right what are the size what is the size of this black box we have to have the number of tokens which is the um context size multiplied by the dimensions right so if you take a look at this black box we have to have the so i''m calculating how many parameters we need to store in memory so if the context size is unit denoted by s we need the context size s multiplied by this dimension so if my number of attention heads are n and if each head dimension is h this will be n into h so s into n into h number of parameters need to be saved and if i have b batches then it will be n into s into h into b and then this will be for the keys as well as the values so this will be multiplied by 2. so if you take a closer look at the size of the kv cache formula you will see that it depends on the context length which is number of my token my number of tokens in the keys and the values matrices multiplied by n into h which is the keys and the values dimension number of attention heads multiplied by the head dimension multiplied by b which is the batch size multiplied by 2 so this first 2 is because i have k and v there are two caches and this second two is because i''m actually calculating uh number of parameters right so these are number of bytes per floating point i''m assuming each parameter i''m assuming i''m calculating the memory of each parameter and i''m assuming each parameter to be floating point to be a 16-bit floating point which is two bytes so every parameter will take two bytes in memory that''s why there is the second two what is this l this l is essentially the number of transformer blocks this transformer block which i showed to you over here every transformer block will have a key value cache and usually uh language models have 12 24 96 or even higher number of transformer blocks so the size of the kv cache also depends on l which is the number of transformer blocks now if you have a deep seek r1 model or v3 the number of transformer blocks they have is 61. the batch size is one the number of attention heads which they have 128 and the dimension of each attention head is 128. the context window is actually 100 000 so if you use this formula to find the size of the kv cache you will see that the size of the kv cache for deep seek r1 or v3 model is 400 gb that''s a huge huge size and why don''t we want such a huge size because the higher the size the more our memory will be overloaded and if let''s say i''m if i am deep seek and i have to store this kv cache in memory which means i have to pay for this memory right it''s like occupying land for every piece of data store we have to pay rent so deep seek has to pay that much amount so then it will charge it in inference but the inference cost which deep seek charges is not that high at all which clearly shows that they are not using this simplified version of kv cache they are doing a whole lot of new modifications after this point which we are going to learn in today''s lecture but this is the dark side of the kv cache it takes up a huge amount of memory takes up a huge amount of memory so it costs more it also it also slows down our other computations because our memory is overloaded imagine what happens if your laptop is filled with memory right your tabs become slower when you are trying to run a piece of code it runs slow your new windows open a bit slow that''s exactly what happens during inference time if you take up a lot of memory so these are the disadvantages of the kv cache now all of the innovations which happened in the attention mechanism which i''m going to discuss in the rest of this lecture are there to solve these disadvantages so after this point people started thinking that okay the kv i of course need the kv kv cache because i want to save these much amount of computations but i need the kv cache but at the same time i want to solve the kv cache memory problem and it''s clearly not being solved with the multi-head attention mechanism so then people started coming up with new ways of solving the kv cache memory problem let''s discuss these new ways next there are essentially two two major things people tried to solve the kv cache problem kv cache memory problem the first is the multi-query attention and the second is the group query attention these are the same diagrams which you will see over here the group query attention and the multi-query attention it''s very difficult to understand anything from these diagrams so i''ve simplified it a bit further here let''s say if you look at the normal multi-head attention right what happens is that if this is wk and this is wv these are the weight drainable weight matrices for keys and the values you will see that these are split into different heads right based on the number of 